One of the main goals of heavy-ion experiments is to study the properties of QGP [16]. The bulk properties of QGP may influence the particle production spectra. The QGP matter can be treated as fluid with collective hydrodynamic flow [17]. Parton energy loss was proposed by Bjorken [18] to be useful to study bulk properties. The gluon radiation induced by the QCD matter may be quite sizable and cause "jet quenching" [19]. Chiral symmetry was predicted to be restored in heavy-ion collisions [20], and model calculations can be compared with experimental data [21]. After creation, the QGP expands and cools down. When the particle production ceases, the system reaches "chemical freeze-out". The freeze-out temperatures and chemical potentials were extracted from particle spectra [22]. When elastic interactions cease, the system reaches "kinetic freeze-out". The freeze-out temperatures and

