As discussed in Chap. 5, the binding energy for muonic hydrogen is orders of magnitude lower than the kinetic energy in heavy-ion experiments. When the atoms are ionized by the beam pipe, the binding energy is canceled by the excitation from atom-beam pipe interaction. As a result, the peak from the two daughter particles are expected to appear at zero net mass, i.e. \(\delta m=m_{pair}-m_{\mu}-m_{hadron}=0\) GeV/\(c^{2}\).

